{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 2,
  "simulation_id": "sim-03",
  "member_id": "johansson-goran",
  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B003",
        "laureates": [
          "Alexandre Blais",
          "Andreas Wallraff",
          "Robert J. Schoelkopf"
        ],
        "citation": "For establishing circuit quantum electrodynamics and demonstrating controllable light–matter interactions in superconducting circuits."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B026",
    "B054"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Circuit QED established a regime in which microwave photons and artificial atoms in superconducting circuits can be coupled and controlled as a quantum optical system. Theoretical work and landmark experiments made this more than a proposed platform; its core phenomena have been reproduced across laboratories and have supported fundamental studies. That record is mature on its own terms, regardless of whether fault-tolerant quantum computing is ultimately achieved. Blais, Wallraff, and Schoelkopf offer a focused theory-and-experiment attribution for that foundational advance.",
    "case_against": "The boundary with earlier superconducting-circuit physics and the 2025 prize remains consequential. The citation must stay with controllable photon–artificial-atom interactions and their establishment, not encompass the later development of processors. Attribution also needs historical support within a field built by many groups. My assessment is that these questions require review, but do not outweigh the identifiable advance and its demonstrated reach.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson favors aberration-corrected electron microscopy for its sharply bounded instrument advance and widespread use, while noting that the roles of Rose, Haider, and Urban need to be distinguished.",
        "response": "The instrument's broad impact is compelling, and the proposed theory-to-corrector account could make a strong case if the historical record supports it. I give circuit QED greater weight because its central contribution is a change in what quantum systems could be experimentally controlled and studied, with a sustained body of independent demonstrations. The attribution and boundary questions remain serious for both proposals; the 2025 prize makes the circuit-QED boundary especially important to settle."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors the KPZ framework and its experimental confirmation as a compact, mature contribution, while questioning whether the proposed names fairly represent the distinct theory and experimental work.",
        "response": "KPZ has an attractive, clearly stated scientific scope, and I agree that its theory-to-experiment attribution deserves close review. Circuit QED is broader as a platform, but the proposed citation can be bounded to the establishment of controllable light–matter interactions, rather than the platform's entire later development. Its experimental maturity and influence across laboratories support keeping it as my preferred configuration."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Historical review should establish how the foundational circuit-QED proposals and demonstrations relate to earlier superconducting-circuit work and to the 2025 prize.",
      "The relative contributions of Blais, Wallraff, and Schoelkopf, including their relation to other foundational contributors, need careful attribution."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "I direct WACQT and work in superconducting circuits, so my field connection warrants review as the chair notes. The supplied profile identifies no direct collaboration or institutional tie to Blais, Wallraff, or Schoelkopf. I have no additional conflict to disclose."
  }
}
